  "notes_context": "These notes were distilled from earlier work in this repository that passed verification. Follow them unless the code or CHANGELOG.md shows they no longer hold.\n- [at-0001] Round money only with round_money: Always round monetary results with ledgerly.money.round_money (half-up to the cent); never use Python's round() or a bare Decimal.quantize(), which use banker's rounding and produce wrong invoice totals.\n  (learned from task E01 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 0x, 0 passed, 0 failed)\n- [at-0002] Normalize money via to_money and round_money: In ledgerly/invoice.py, convert any monetary input with to_money() and store it rounded with round_money(); compare against ZERO from ledgerly.money rather than raw numbers.\n  (learned from task E02 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 0x, 0 passed, 0 failed)\n- [at-0003] Raise BillingError with snake_case codes: Reject invalid input in ledgerly/invoice.py by raising BillingError(code, detail) with codes like \"invalid_state\", \"invalid_quantity\" and \"negative_amount\", matching existing functions such as issue_invoice.\n  (learned from task E02 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 0x, 0 passed, 0 failed)\n- [at-0004] Reject bools when validating integer quantities: When validating integer quantities in ledgerly/invoice.py, explicitly exclude bool via isinstance(quantity, bool) before the int check, since True/False are ints in Python.\n  (learned from task E02 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 0x, 0 passed, 0 failed)",
